ercial-grade parts on their truck, the fuel, the insurance, the professional-grade tools that get the job done right, and the licensing and bonding that protect you if something goes wrong. A solo plumber's overhead can easily be over $100-200/hour before they pay themselves a dime. 2. **Desperation pricing is a terrible long-term business model.** A plumber who overcharges victims for a quick score won't stay in business. Their entire livelihood depends on repeat customers and, more importantly, word-of-mouth reputation. In the age of Nextdoor, Google reviews, and neighborhood Facebook groups, a single story of "this guy charged my elderly mom $2000 for a $200 fix" can be devastating. A smart plumber knows a fair price on an emergency call builds a customer for life. The bad actors absolutely exist—as they do in every single profession—but the stereotype imagines them as the rule. ### Why accepting this stereotype actually hurts you. If you go into the interaction believing the plumber is a barely-educated predator, you'll likely communicate suspicion through your tone and questions. That puts them on the defensive and poisons any chance of a collaborative conversation. You might miss that their "crazy price" includes a high-efficiency pump with a 10-year warranty, while the cheaper quote is for a basic model that'll fail in three years. You could also get hustled more easily by someone who *does* have bad intentions, because they'll confirm your bias with charm, while a genuinely skilled pro might come off as brusque when they feel unfairly judged. The feeling is real, but the explanation you've been given is a stereotype that will steer you wrong. You don't need to trust blindly, and you definitely don't need to accept a price that feels off. You need a reliable method to cut through the noise. So, forget the high school grades. Forget the assumption of greed. Just use the checklist: - **Get three itemized quotes.** This kills the "crazy price" mystery instantly.